# Line endings are normalised in the repository and checked out as LF on every
# platform, Windows included.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the one that matters here.
#
# 1. The noise. Git was emitting "CRLF will be replaced by LF" on nearly every
#    `git add`, for every file, forever. A warning that fires on all traffic is
#    a warning nobody reads, so the day a real one appears -- a merge conflict,
#    a file that cannot be parsed -- it is already background.
#
# 2. The bytes. The drift suite reads committed HTML fixtures and asserts that
#    configured anchor strings appear in them. A fixture that is LF in the
#    repository and CRLF in a Windows working tree is a different byte sequence
#    on the machine running the tests than on the machine that committed them.
#    Python's universal newlines hide that for text reads, which is exactly why
#    it is dangerous: it works right up until something reads bytes, and then it
#    fails on one platform only. Identical bytes everywhere removes the whole
#    class rather than relying on every reader to be well-behaved.
#
# `text=auto` rather than `text`, so git still detects binary content and leaves
# it alone. `eol=lf` is what forces the working copy to match the repository
# instead of the platform default.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the file types this project actually contains, so the intent
# survives someone reading only this section. `*.py text` also stops git from
# treating a Python file as binary because of a stray byte in a docstring, which
# is how a Latin-1 comment once gets committed as-is and then fails to parse on
# a machine with a different default encoding.
*.py      text eol=lf diff=python
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.jsonl   text eol=lf
*.html    text eol=lf
*.css     text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.cfg     text eol=lf
*.ini     text eol=lf

# Binary, and marked as such deliberately rather than left to `auto` detection.
# Screenshots and Playwright traces are written next to the runtime data
# directory and must never be line-ending translated: a translated trace zip is
# a corrupt trace zip, and a "corrupt" artifact sends someone hunting for a bug
# in the capture code that is not there.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.webp    binary
*.ico     binary
*.zip     binary
*.trace.zip binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.eot     binary

# Nothing in this repository is generated, and nothing is worth merging without
# conflict. Marking the generated shapes as such keeps git from offering to
# merge two people's checkpoints, which is a merge that cannot mean anything.
*.checkpoint.jsonl linguist-generated=true -merge
